From: Eric Tanner <eric.tanner@harvia.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: vt-sbc-am62l: disable the unused SD card slot in Linux

The product has nothing in the SD slot and is flashed over USB (flash-tools),
yet the kernel kept the slot powered (vdd_sd_dv, GPIO0_123 SD_PWR) and,
because the slot has no card-detect line ("broken-cd"), polled it for a
card every second for the life of the system - measured as ~3600 mmc1
interrupts in the first five minutes and a permanently resident irq
thread. Disable the controller in Linux; the rail then drops with it.
U-Boot's DT copy is left alone so an SD card still works for recovery
boots.

Signed-off-by: Eric Tanner <eric.tanner@harvia.com>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/ti/vt-sbc-am62l.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/ti/vt-sbc-am62l.dtsi
@@ -318,7 +318,7 @@
 	vqmmc-supply = <&vdd_sd_dv>;
 	pinctrl-names = "default";
 	pinctrl-0 = <&main_mmc1_pins_default>;
-	status = "okay";
+	status = "disabled";
 	bootph-all;
 	broken-cd;
 	no-sdio;
